---
import CaseStudy from "../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";

/*
 * Alma — e-commerce web app built for a client.
 * Facts come from the coder agent's notes (~/Documents/agent-org/coder/alma-notes.md),
 * which were read from the private repo's code and git history.
 * Facts still missing are marked TODO(user) and rendered as visible <mark class="todo">
 * so they cannot ship unnoticed. Client name, live dates, team/role and outcomes are
 * deliberately NOT stated until the user confirms them. No link to the app repo
 * (private, contains secrets) — UD 2026-11-12.
 */
---

<CaseStudy
  eyebrow="E-commerce web app · Client work · 2022–2024"
  title="Alma: an online shop with a 3D Secure card checkout"
  outcome="An online shop built for a client: catalogue, bag, accounts and a 3D Secure card checkout on a serverless AWS backend."
  role="Front end and backend"
  timeline="Sep 2022 – Jul 2024"
  tools={["React (CRA)", "MUI", "Formik + Yup", "AWS Amplify", "Lambda + Express", "MongoDB", "S3 + CloudFront", "iyzico (3D Secure)", "Nodemailer"]}
  liveUrl="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/demo/"
  liveLabel="Live demo"
  noindex
>
  <h2>Problem &amp; constraints</h2>
  <p>
    Alma is an online shop for a small fashion label selling resort wear: cotton robes,
    gauze shirts, crochet sets. The client designs and produces the clothes and wanted
    one place to sell them and to present the brand, so I built a custom storefront
    rather than fitting the label into a template.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two constraints shaped it. The brand's look came as finished page artwork, and the
    shop had to keep it. And payments had to work with Turkish cards, which almost
    always go through 3D Secure.
  </p>
  <p>
    The visual design was not mine: a designer I worked alongside delivered the page
    artwork, the type and the layouts as finished designs, and my job was to build them
    faithfully. It was the first time I watched a design process up close — how a screen
    was decided, questioned and revised before any code existed — and it is one of the
    projects where my interest in UI/UX design started.
  </p>

  <Figure
    src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/home-desktop.webp"
    alt="Alma home page at desktop width: the brand wordmark centred over a slim navigation (shop, collections, notebook, search, account, shopping bag) and a full-width photo of a model in a striped robe on a beach at sunset."
    caption="The home page from the demo. The layout is the brand's own artwork, one image per breakpoint."
  />

  <h2>Process</h2>
  <p>
    The front end is a React single-page app (Create React App) with MUI for the grid,
    plain CSS per page with four breakpoints, and Formik with Yup for the checkout
    forms. The backend is an AWS Amplify project: one Lambda running an Express API
    behind API Gateway, MongoDB for products, users, carts and payment results, S3 with
    CloudFront for product images, and Nodemailer for the e-mails. The work ran from
    September 2022 to July 2024, with most of it between September 2022 and spring 2023.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shop is no longer online. The demo on this page is the same front end, built
    against a small mock of the API: nine invented products, a stand-in Instagram feed,
    and a simulated bank page. The UI, CSS and copy are unchanged.
  </p>

  <div class="shots">
    <figure>
      <img src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/shop-desktop.webp" alt="Shop grid at desktop width: Filter on the left, Sort by on the right, four product photos with names and prices in Turkish lira." loading="lazy" width="1440" height="900" />
      <figcaption>Shop grid with filter (material, colour, type) and sort.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/product-sidebar-desktop.webp" alt="Product page with the bag drawer open on the right: a tall photo of the striped robe, and a small bag summary with a Checkout button." loading="lazy" width="1440" height="900" />
      <figcaption>On desktop, Add to cart opens the bag as a side drawer; on a phone it goes to the bag page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Key decisions</h2>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>1 — 3D Secure inside the shop, not a redirect away from it</h3>
    <p>
      Checkout runs in steps: contact and address, shipping, card, then the bank's 3D
      Secure page. The API starts the payment with iyzico and returns the bank's page,
      which the shop shows in a frame on its own checkout. When the bank is done, the
      result page tells the shop through a message whether the payment went through.
      Only a success message empties the bag; a cancel or a failure sends the customer
      back to it with everything still there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>2 — Stock changes once, when the payment succeeds</h3>
    <p>
      The payment callback completes the payment, lowers the stock for what was bought,
      stores the result and sends the order e-mail, in that order. A product at zero
      stock shows a "Sold out" overlay in the grid.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> updating stock on the server every time an item was
      added to or removed from a bag. The calls are still in the code, commented out;
      a bag is not a sale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>3 — Guest checkout without an account</h3>
    <p>
      Signing up is optional. A guest's e-mail and address are saved as an unregistered
      buyer with a temporary token, so the order still has someone to belong to. The
      form checks while you type whether the e-mail already has an account and offers a
      sign-in instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>4 — The brand's artwork, kept as artwork</h3>
    <p>
      The home and "notebook" pages are the brand's page designs exported as one image
      per breakpoint, with invisible links placed over the products in the picture.
      That kept the editorial look exact. The cost is weight: the home page artwork is
      several megabytes at desktop size.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>5 — The shop grid loads four products first</h3>
    <p>
      The first request asks for four products so the top of the grid fills quickly;
      the full list follows and is merged in by id. Product photos come from S3
      through CloudFront in two sizes: small for the grid, large for the product page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>The checkout on a phone</h3>
  <div class="phones">
    <figure>
      <img src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/bag-mobile.webp" alt="Bag on a phone: one striped robe with quantity 2, subtotal, free shipping and total in Turkish lira, the code WELCOME10 applied, and a Checkout button." loading="lazy" width="375" height="812" />
      <figcaption>Bag with the discount code applied.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/checkout-mobile.webp" alt="Checkout step one on a phone: contact e-mail, a Login button for existing accounts, and the shipping address fields filled with demo data." loading="lazy" width="375" height="812" />
      <figcaption>Step 1, contact and address. Guests do not need an account.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/assets/payment-mobile.webp" alt="Payment step on a phone: a note about the iyzico card payment, a billing address choice set to same as shipping, and the card fields." loading="lazy" width="375" height="812" />
      <figcaption>The card step, before the bank's 3D Secure page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Try the demo</h3>
  <p>
    The demo runs on mock data and makes no payment. Add something to the bag, use the
    code <strong>WELCOME10</strong>, and any 16-digit card number takes you to a demo
    3D Secure page. Any e-mail and password signs you in. <a href="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/demo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the demo full-screen</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="demo" id="demo">
    <div class="browser">
      <iframe src="/alma-estore-project-983d18e6-0e00-4b3c-8a25-0c3f414c293f/demo/" title="Alma online shop, clickable demo on mock data" loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Outcome &amp; what's next</h2>
  <p>
    <mark class="todo">TODO(user): how long the shop was live, whether real orders went through it, and anything true about how it was used or what the client said. No numbers until confirmed.</mark>
  </p>
  <p>
    Looking back at the code, a few things I would do differently: keep one
    free-shipping threshold in the financial values instead of two hard-coded ones;
    configure Amplify once instead of in several components; and serve the page
    artwork as compressed, responsive images. The checkout flow and the one-stock-update
    rule I would keep.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  /* Desktop screenshots: two up, stacked on phones */
  .shots {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  .shots figure,
  .wide,
  .phones figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .wide {
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  .shots img,
  .wide img,
  .phones img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .phones img {
    border-radius: 18px;
  }
  .shots figcaption,
  .wide figcaption,
  .phones figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Phone screenshots: three up, scaled to the prose measure */
  .phones {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .shots,
    .phones {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .phones figure {
      max-width: 375px;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  /* Live demo in a browser-shaped frame; a 1280 px wide page scaled to the frame */
  .demo {
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  .browser {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1280 / 800;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 0 20px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18);
  }
  .browser iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 1280px;
    height: 800px;
    border: 0;
    transform-origin: top left;
  }
  /* Phone width: show the demo at its own mobile layout instead of a tiny desktop */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .browser {
      width: min(100%, 390px);
      margin-inline: auto;
      aspect-ratio: 390 / 844;
      border-radius: 28px;
    }
    .browser iframe {
      width: 390px;
      height: 844px;
    }
  }

  /* Missing facts — visible on purpose until the user fills them in */
  mark.todo {
    background: none;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Scale the demo iframe (1280 desktop / 390 phone) to the frame's actual width
  const fit = () => {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".browser").forEach((frame) => {
      const f = frame.querySelector("iframe");
      if (!f) return;
      const base = matchMedia("(max-width: 48rem)").matches ? 390 : 1280;
      f.style.transform = `scale(${frame.clientWidth / base})`;
    });
  };
  fit();
  addEventListener("resize", fit);
</script>
